{"id":"known:df316a3d1cbbe5faeb20225704c4ceb6","url":"/archive/known/?id=df316a3d1cbbe5faeb20225704c4ceb6","source":"known","type":"replies","title":"@chadsansing Went in read the road map. Saw you are doing case studies. First step then is deciding criteria for case section. For ex You have the pre","summary":"@chadsansing Went in read the road map. Saw you are doing case studies. First step then is deciding criteria for case section. For ex You have the pre-post data. You could select X cases in each quartile, select X cases who showed Y growth and compare to group who showed Z growth. You could pick them a priori. When doing comparative case study analysis for t","date":"2018-04-10","published":"2018-04-10T13:35:17+00:00","author":"Greg McVerry","reply_to":null,"tags":[],"access":"PUBLIC","publish_status":"published"}
{"id":"known:6fe3028fec48420c5c17007ee69d9c15","url":"/archive/known/?id=6fe3028fec48420c5c17007ee69d9c15","source":"known","type":"articles","title":"Giving up the CMS would have to be #HigherEd first step into #IndieWeb","summary":"Most universities in #HigherEd have their general web strategy all wrong. The \"canonical url\" should be light weight. A predictable and accessible navigation with fewest pages possible that render HTML from databases that faculty populate (Yes classic garbage in, garbage out, but that is a supervision problem and not a technical one). Faculty and departments","date":"2018-04-10","published":"2018-04-10T13:20:24+00:00","author":"Greg McVerry","reply_to":null,"tags":["HigherEd"],"access":"PUBLIC","publish_status":"published"}
